  "answer_text": "Here's a breakdown of typical linebacker jersey numbers in the NFL:\n\n### Current NFL Linebacker Number Rules (Post-2021 Expansion)\n\nLinebackers are currently allowed to wear numbers **1\u201359** and **90\u201399**.\n\n---\n\n### Most Traditional/Common Ranges\n\n- **50\u201359** are the most traditionally associated numbers for linebackers (shared with centers).\n- **90\u201399** are also commonly worn by linebackers and defensive linemen.\n\n---\n\n### How the Rules Evolved Over Time\n\n1. In **1984**, the NFL allowed linebackers to wear jersey numbers in the **90\u201399** range, since more teams were making use of the 3\u20134 defense and were quickly exhausting numbers for linebackers, who previously were only allowed to wear numbers in the **50\u201359** range.\n\n2. The NFL Competition Committee made another change in **2015**, allowing linebackers to use numbers **40\u201349** for the first time.\n\n3. In **2021**, NFL owners passed a rule expanding available numbers, allowing linebackers to wear **1\u201379** and **90\u201399**. This was later refined, and the current rule settles linebackers at **1\u201359** and **90\u201399**.\n\n---\n\n### Quick Summary Table\n\n| Number Range | Traditionally Associated With |\n|---|---|\n| 1\u201319 | Rarely used by LBs (now allowed) |\n| 40\u201349 | Allowed since 2015 |\n| **50\u201359** | **Most classic LB numbers** |\n| 60\u201379 | Not typically LB numbers |\n| **90\u201399** | **Very common for LBs** |\n\nSo if you're thinking of a \"classic\" linebacker number, you're most likely looking at something in the **50s** (like 51, 52, 55, 58) or the **90s** (like 90, 93, 94, 99).",
